dsh-code-review — Debian/Ubuntu package notes
=============================================

What this package installs
--------------------------
  /usr/lib/dsh/plugins/code-review/   the plugin package root (lib/)
  /usr/share/doc/dsh-code-review/     this note, copyright, changelog,
                                      BUILD-INFO.json

The package root contains package.json, cordis.patch.yml, and lib/ (the
compiled Host entry, its declarations, and the browser bundle), plus locale/,
icon.svg, and a private node_modules/ with the pinned schemastery schema
dependency. That directory is the unit DeepSeek Harness installs: the
Web Plugins page ("Add plugin") and `dsh plugin add <path>` both read it.

The package does not modify user profiles and does not start a service. It
also does not install `gh`, Node.js, or DeepSeek Harness itself; it Depends on
deepseek-harness (>= @DSH_MIN_VERSION@) and Recommends the GitHub CLI.

Enabling the plugin (per user, not with sudo)
---------------------------------------------
  dsh plugin --profile web add /usr/lib/dsh/plugins/code-review

Or, in the Web UI: Plugins -> Add plugin -> enter
/usr/lib/dsh/plugins/code-review.

Installation enables the bundle in the profile. A running Host picks it up
through HMR when the profile uses live reload; otherwise restart the Web UI:

  systemctl --user restart dsh-web

Do not run these commands with sudo: the profile belongs to the user, and
root-owned files under ~/.dsh break the user service. A Host plugin runs
in-process with that user's privileges and can read its credentials, so
install only packages you trust.

Upgrading
---------
`apt install` replaces the directory in place. The profile links to the
directory, not to a version, so no reinstall is needed; restart the Web UI to
load the new code (or let HMR reload it).

Removing
--------
Remove the bundle from every profile that enabled it first:

  dsh plugin --profile web remove @PLUGIN_NAME@

Then remove the package:

  sudo apt remove dsh-code-review

Removing the package first leaves a dangling link in the profile. DeepSeek
Harness then reports the bundle as skipped, and the `dsh plugin remove`
command above still clears it. `apt purge` removes only the files listed in
the package; no user data is touched.

Usage
-----
After enabling and restarting:

  /code-review                       review the open pull request of the branch
  /code-review 123                   review PR #123 in the session repository
  /code-review owner/repo#123        review #123 in another repository
  /code-review https://github.com/owner/repo/pull/123

The command returns immediately and the review runs in a background subagent.
`gh auth login` must be completed for the user running the Host.

On the Web client, open Plugins and select Code review. The settings card
on the package page edits the report language, finding limit, and context
budgets; the same form is also on the `code-review` row. The card saves through the Harness settings
service and applies to the next review without restarting the Host. The remaining
deployment limits stay in the profile's cordis.patch.yml.

License
-------
MIT. The full text is installed as /usr/share/doc/dsh-code-review/copyright
and /usr/share/doc/dsh-code-review/LICENSE.

Source
------
Upstream: @PLUGIN_REPO_URL@
The exact version and commit used for this build are recorded in
/usr/share/doc/dsh-code-review/BUILD-INFO.json and in the package's
X-DSH-Plugin-Commit control field.
